+# Soft 404 on pages that load fine
+
+## Cause
+
+Googlebot renders the page with JavaScript and fetches the data it needs from the site’s internal API (e.g. `/v1/...`). The **robots.txt** file contained a rule:
+
+```
+Disallow: /v1/
+```
+
+Because this rule blocks the API path, Google is not allowed to retrieve the JSON that populates the page. When the fetch fails, the application displays its *“NOT FOUND — No story lives at this address”* screen with an HTTP 200 response. Google interprets that as a **soft 404** – a page that returns a successful status code but has no substantive content.
+
+## Fix
+
+Update **robots.txt** so that the public read endpoints needed for page rendering are **allowed**, while keeping the POST‑only and per‑user endpoints blocked with more specific rules. The longest‑match rule wins, and `Allow` beats a tie, so the file should look something like:
+
+```
+User-agent: *
+# Allow the read‑only API endpoints used by the pages
+Allow: /v1/stories
+
+# Keep the generic block for the rest of the API
+Disallow: /v1/
+
+# Block any per‑user or admin endpoints with longer paths
+Disallow: /v1/stories/*/reproduced
+Disallow: /v1/admin/
+```
+
+After deploying the updated `robots.txt`:
+1. Verify it’s live (`curl -s https://<site>/robots.txt`).
+2. In Search Console request a recrawl of the affected URLs.
+3. Use the URL Inspection tool – the pages should no longer be reported as *Soft 404* and will move to *Crawled – currently not indexed* and eventually to *Indexed*.
+
+By allowing the necessary API calls, Google can render the full content, eliminating the soft 404 classification.
